# Claude Code settings. Neither file is committed.
#
# The shared project-level settings.json used to be tracked, on the reasoning
# that a permissions allowlist is team config worth sharing. It is not worth
# what it costs here: app_location is "/" on all four workflows, so the repo
# root IS the deployed site, and staticwebapp.config.json only gates /, /*.html
# and /api/*. Verified against production -- GET /.claude/settings.json
# returned 200 with the file's contents, unauthenticated, on both instances.
#
# The contents are not credentials, but they do list internal paths, resource
# group and database names, and the shape of our tooling. None of that belongs
# on a public URL.
.claude/

# Reference material: vendor documentation, and the spec files and feedback
# decks that come over from the team. Kept locally, never committed.
#
# The original reason was that app_location is "/" on all four workflows, so
# the repo root IS the deployed site, and staticwebapp.config.json gated only
# /, /*.html and /api/* -- anything else at the root was served anonymously.
# That hole is closed: there is now a catch-all "/*" route requiring
# authentication, placed last, and the config records which files were
# verified returning 200 with no session before it existed.
#
# These stay ignored for the OTHER reason, which no route rule can fix: this
# repository is PUBLIC. A committed file is readable on github.com by anyone,
# and git history is immutable -- deleting it later does not unpublish it. So
# client specs, vendor documentation and internal workbooks do not go in.
#
# Wildcards rather than exact names on purpose: the recurring case is the NEXT
# spec document arriving, and it should be caught without anyone having to
# remember this file. No tracked file has any of these extensions.
VNDLY API Docs.pdf
*.pdf
*.docx
*.doc
*.xlsx
*.xls
*.pptx
*.zip
